From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Rowley <johnrowleyster@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 13:04:47 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(miniwin): D3DRM light range, spot cones, enable frames
 and material terms

IDirect3DRMLight keeps its colour, range, umbra, penumbra, attenuation and
enable frame, with D3DRM's defaults (range 256, umbra 0.4, penumbra 0.5,
attenuation 1, 0, 0), and gains the matching getters. SetColor reads the
colour as 0xAARRGGBB; the viewport used to read the packed colour with red
and blue swapped.

SceneLight carries the range, attenuation and cone after its first 48
bytes, which stay the layout the GPU backends upload; the SDL3 GPU backend
copies that prefix into its own shader struct, and the DirectX 9 and
OpenGL 1 bridges mirror the new fields.

The viewport scopes a light with an enable frame to that frame's
hierarchy: it walks the scene with a mask of the lights that reach each
frame and pushes the renderer the matching set before each draw.

The software backend applies range, attenuation and Direct3D's linear spot
falloff to positional lights. D3DRM's umbra and penumbra are angles from
the spot's axis, half of Direct3D's theta and phi: LEGO Rock Raiders'
first level matches the shipped game's frame that way, and is far too dark
with full cone angles. Immediate-mode draws are lit with Direct3D's
material formula: emissive, plus ambient light times the material's
ambient colour, plus each light's diffuse, and specular added after the
texture stage when SPECULARENABLE is set. IDirect3DRMMaterial2 keeps its
emissive and specular colours.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

